# {{ name }}

{{ description }}

A WhatsApp bot as a kdeps workflow. The `whatsapp_webhook` listener receives Meta's webhook, `llm` answers the message, and `reply` sends the answer with the `whatsapp_post` connector.

```text
Meta -> GET  /whatsapp -> hub.challenge (verify token matches) - no run
Meta -> POST /whatsapp (signed) -> 202 -> llm -> reply (whatsapp_post: /messages)
```

## Quick Start

1. Give kdeps the credentials from your Meta app (WhatsApp > API Setup):
   ```bash
   export WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=1234567890     # the sending number's ID
   export WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=EAAG...           # or: kdeps secret set whatsapp_access_token
   export WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=pick-any-string   # or: kdeps secret set whatsapp_verify_token
   export WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=abc123...           # App settings > Basic; or: kdeps secret set whatsapp_app_secret
   ```

2. Run it and expose port 16396 over HTTPS (Meta only calls HTTPS URLs):
   ```bash
   kdeps run workflow.yaml
   # e.g. cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:16396
   ```

3. In Meta's webhook settings, set the callback URL to `https://<host>/whatsapp` and the verify token to `WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN`. Subscribe to `messages`.

## How it works

- A `GET /whatsapp` gets `hub.challenge` back only when `hub.verify_token` equals `WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN`.
- A `POST /whatsapp` must carry a valid `X-Hub-Signature-256` (HMAC-SHA256 of the body with `WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET`). A forged POST gets 401 and starts no run.
- Status callbacks (sent, delivered, read) have no text message: `llm` and `reply` are skipped.
- The first text message of each notification is answered.

Change the model or persona in `resources/llm.yaml`. The Graph API version is in the `path` of `resources/reply.yaml`.
